      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты с чего взял?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where did you get that?' In Russian, 'you' leads into 'got' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ещё один нелегал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Another illegal.' Notice the word order: 'more' comes first, and 'illegal immigrant' finishes the sentence. Then Chef Viktor replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Господи, куда вас девать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lord, where to put you?' Chef Viktor puts 'Good Lord' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'where to'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы отсидитесь в туалете.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You will sit in the toilet.' Listen for the word meaning 'you' right alongside 'to sit out' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потом уйдёте через вход.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then leave through the entrance.' In Russian, 'then' leads into 'entrance' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And /  / jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Они дежурят на входе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are on guard at the entrance.' /  / starts with 'they' and pairs it with 'to be on duty' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сядет в зал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He will sit in the hall.' Chef Viktor puts 'to sit down' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'the hall'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сходи в бар, возьми одежду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go to the bar, take clothes.' The key word here means 'go', which Chef Viktor combines with 'bar'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Терпи, давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Endure, come on.' Here 'bear' sets up the idea, while 'let's' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Поздравляю вас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I congratulate you.' A compact expression built around 'congratulations' and 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "У вас все чисто.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything is clean with you.' You'll hear 'you' all the time in the show, here connected with 'clean'. Then Chef Viktor replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я же вам сразу сказал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you right away.' Chef Viktor uses the polite plural 'you' here alongside 'I', keeping a formal or respectful tone.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Я не имею права верить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have no right to believe.' Notice the word order: 'I' comes first, and 'to believe' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы профессионалы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are professionals.' Here 'we' sets up the idea, while 'professionals' comes at the end for emphasis.",
